/**
 * Reolink Persistent Event Subscription Test Tool (STANDALONE -- NOT part of the Reolink Integration app)
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * WHAT THIS IS FOR
 * ============================================================================
 * Tests real-time local event delivery for a battery-class Reolink device
 * (camera or doorbell) sitting behind a Home Hub/NVR, using Reolink's
 * proprietary "Baichuan" TCP protocol (port 9000 by default).
 *
 * VALIDATED MECHANISM (as of 2026-08-06): a PERSISTENT event subscription
 * (cmd_id=31 on push channel 251), kept alive with periodic cmd_id=93
 * keepalives, with real events (cmd_id=33 motion/AI/visitor, cmd_id=145
 * sleep) arriving as ongoing pushes over the same open connection. Live-
 * tested against a Home Hub Pro watching the battery doorbell: cleanly
 * captured a full approach -> ring -> departure sequence, connection stayed
 * up ~5 min with keepalives acking on schedule, no drops.
 *
 * NOT NEEDED FOR THIS PROJECT: a one-shot webhook registration (cmd_id=807),
 * and the companion local HTTP receiver app that would go with it. That was
 * the original plan but is now known to be unnecessary -- see "WHY NO
 * WEBHOOK" below. The startEventSubscription()/stopEventSubscription()
 * commands below are the real, complete mechanism; testWebhookFlow() is
 * kept only as a reference/comparison path, not something production needs.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * WHY NO WEBHOOK FOR THIS (HUB/NVR-RELAYED) CASE -- confirmed by reading
 * reolink_aio's own subscribe_events()/get_host_data() source directly
 * ============================================================================
 * reolink_aio (the officially-backed library HA uses) treats webhook as a
 * SUPPLEMENT to the persistent subscription, not an alternative to it -- and
 * only for STANDALONE battery devices, never for Hub/NVR-relayed channels:
 *
 *   is_battery = NOT is_nvr AND device reports a battery
 *
 * A Hub/NVR is always is_nvr=True, so any channel behind it is ALWAYS
 * is_battery=False, regardless of whether the underlying camera itself runs
 * on battery. Standalone (non-Hub) battery devices get BOTH the cmd_id=31
 * subscription AND a webhook, because reolink_aio deliberately closes their
 * TCP connection after ~5s idle to conserve the device's own battery --
 * webhook exists purely to catch events after that intentional disconnect.
 * The library doesn't even ASK a Hub for webhook-ability (cmd_id=806) --
 * that check only runs when is_battery is true. This is why this Home Hub
 * Pro's ability response had no webhook field: not a firmware gap, the
 * library never queries a Hub for it in the first place.
 *
 * Since a Hub is mains-powered, there's no battery-conservation reason to
 * ever close its persistent connection -- so cmd_id=31 subscription is the
 * complete, permanent mechanism for any Hub/NVR-relayed channel. No webhook,
 * no local HTTP receiver, no companion app.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * WHAT PRODUCTION WILL NEED (not yet built here -- this is still a test tool)
 * ============================================================================
 * Same is_battery-style split applies to the production Reolink Integration
 * app going forward:
 *   - Hub/NVR-relayed channels (this Home Hub Pro's doorbell/cameras): use
 *     ONLY the persistent cmd_id=31 subscription, exactly as validated here.
 *   - Any FUTURE standalone battery device NOT behind a Hub would need the
 *     webhook path in addition (cmd_id=807/806) plus a local HTTP receiver
 *     endpoint (an app, via mappings{} -- a driver alone can't listen), to
 *     catch events after its own battery-saving disconnect. Not built or
 *     tested yet -- no non-Hub-relayed battery hardware on hand to verify
 *     against.
 *
 * Remaining work before this logic moves into production: reconnect/
 * resilience after a real connection drop, confirming the post-subscribe
 * state churn (sleep flapping connect/null/connect, stray cmd_ids) is
 * consistent across fresh subscribes, multiple back-to-back events, and
 * behavior across a Hubitat hub reboot.
 *
 * Completely standalone -- own login/session, no dependency on the
 * production Reolink Integration app, cannot affect any existing source or
 * child device. No credentials are ever logged.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * PROTOCOL NOTES (for future reference / debugging)
 * ============================================================================
 * Every detail below was confirmed directly from reolink_aio's source
 * (baichuan.py, util.py, base_protocol.py, xmls.py) -- not guessed.
 *
 * Header (little-endian), 20 or 24 bytes depending on message class:
 *   bytes[0:4]   magic: f0 de bc 0a (HEADER_MAGIC)
 *   bytes[4:8]   cmd_id
 *   bytes[8:12]  body length
 *   bytes[12:16] message id: byte 0 = channel id (250=host), bytes 1-3 = counter
 *   bytes[16:18] on OUTGOING messages: always "0000". On INCOMING messages:
 *                the actual encryption-type marker (00dd=unencrypted,
 *                01dd/12dd=Baichuan-XOR-encrypted, 02dd/03dd=AES-encrypted)
 *   bytes[18:20] message class: "1464"=24-byte header (normal), "1466"=20-byte
 *                header (some responses), "1465"=used ONLY for the very first
 *                bare nonce request (20-byte header, no payload_offset field)
 *   bytes[20:24] payload_offset (ONLY present for 24-byte "1464"/"0000" header)
 *
 * Two ciphers, used at different stages -- NOT interchangeable:
 *   - Baichuan's own simple XOR cipher (encrypt/decrypt are the same
 *     function, XOR is symmetric): byte ^ XML_KEY[(offset+idx) % 8] ^ offset,
 *     where offset = the channel id (250 for host-level commands). Used ONLY
 *     for the login step itself.
 *   - AES-CFB-128, fixed IV "0123456789abcdef" (literal ASCII bytes, NOT the
 *     IV found during the earlier HTTP-side encryption investigation -- a
 *     different fixed IV for this different protocol). Key = first 16 chars
 *     of md5Modern("{nonce}-{password}"), used as raw UTF-8 bytes. Used for
 *     every authenticated command AFTER login succeeds.
 *
 * "md5Modern" is NOT a plain MD5 hex digest: take the standard 32-char lower
 * case MD5 hex digest, truncate to the first 31 characters, uppercase it.
 * (Then, for the AES key specifically, truncate THAT 31-char result to 16.)
 *
 * Full one-shot sequence for a battery device:
 *   1. cmd_id=1, no body (bare header, message class 1465) -> get a nonce
 *      from the response, derive the AES key
 *   2. cmd_id=1, Baichuan-XOR-encrypted body (LOGIN_XML with md5Modern-hashed
 *      username/password) -> log in
 *   3. cmd_id=199, no body, AES-encrypted -> confirm the "webhook" ability
 *      field is present (only meaningful once already known to be battery)
 *   4. cmd_id=807, AES-encrypted body (WEBHOOK_PUSH XML with our URL) ->
 *      register the webhook
 *   5. cmd_id=806, no body, AES-encrypted -> verify the registration took
 *   6. cmd_id=2, AES-encrypted body (LOGOUT_XML, plaintext creds -- fine,
 *      channel is encrypted) -> logout, then close the socket
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * HOW TO USE
 * ============================================================================
 *  1. Drivers Code > New Driver > paste this in > Save.
 *  2. Devices > Add Device > Virtual > "Reolink Webhook Test Tool" > Save.
 *  3. Fill in Preferences: the Home Hub Pro's IP, Baichuan port (9000
 *     default), username, password, and the webhook URL (leave blank for
 *     now until the companion receiver app exists -- registration will
 *     still complete, you just won't see the resulting events yet).
 *  4. Run "Start Event Subscription" on the Commands tab to test the real,
 *     validated mechanism -- watch Logs, then walk in front of / ring the
 *     device to confirm live events. Run "Stop Event Subscription" when
 *     done. "Test Webhook Flow" is kept only as a reference/comparison path
 *     (see WHY NO WEBHOOK above) -- not needed for a Hub-relayed device.
 * ============================================================================
 */
